WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.292 --> 00:00:02.627
Maybe this was a mistake.

00:00:11.136 --> 00:00:14.014
The United States has always had 
a love hate relationship

00:00:14.014 --> 00:00:15.306
when it comes to train travel.

00:00:15.682 --> 00:00:18.560
The country was literally built
on the backs of the railroads

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:20.437
and the US used to have the biggest 

00:00:20.437 --> 00:00:23.189
and the most well-funded 
rail network in the world.

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:25.692
But if you ask anybody 
that's taken the Amtrak...

00:00:25.734 --> 00:00:28.111
they'll tell you that that's not exactly 
the case anymore.

00:00:29.904 --> 00:00:32.699
The US has fallen woefully behind 
the rest of the world

00:00:32.699 --> 00:00:33.950
when it comes to train travel.

00:00:34.242 --> 00:00:35.785
So what the heck happened?

00:00:36.327 --> 00:00:38.455
How did the United States 
get so far behind?

00:00:38.455 --> 00:00:40.415
And does it have any chance 
of catching up?

00:00:41.374 --> 00:00:43.501
To find out the answer to that, 
I'm going to take the Amtrak

00:00:43.501 --> 00:00:45.962
from here in Los Angeles all the way 
to New York City.

00:00:46.337 --> 00:00:48.048
And I'm going to have plenty 
of time to think about it

00:00:48.048 --> 00:00:51.051
since it's a... 70-hour journey.

00:00:51.551 --> 00:00:52.677
I guess I better get started.

00:01:12.322 --> 00:01:13.490
The entire length of my journey

00:01:13.490 --> 00:01:16.618
from Los Angeles to New York 
is about four days long...

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:18.703
and for the first leg of my journey

00:01:18.703 --> 00:01:20.580
there weren't any sleeper cars available.

00:01:20.872 --> 00:01:22.373
So, this is what my living space

00:01:22.373 --> 00:01:24.084
looks like for the next two days

00:01:24.084 --> 00:01:26.628
which as somebody that's 
[BEEP] years old

00:01:26.628 --> 00:01:28.171
should be really good for my back.

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:30.715
The journey I'm taking 
is pretty different

00:01:30.715 --> 00:01:32.759
than it would have been about 
a hundred years ago.

00:01:32.926 --> 00:01:35.804
To see the differences, 
let's take a look at this US map.

00:01:36.221 --> 00:01:41.392
The year is 1916 and there's about 
254,000 miles of railroads

00:01:41.392 --> 00:01:43.686
the highest number there's ever been 
in this country.

00:01:44.354 --> 00:01:48.024
The Pennsylvania railroad, or the Pennsy,
as it was referred to...

00:01:48.566 --> 00:01:49.359
I feel like that nickname

00:01:49.359 --> 00:01:51.069
could have probably used 
a little more work.

00:01:51.402 --> 00:01:53.446
Nevertheless, it was the largest corporation

00:01:53.446 --> 00:01:54.989
in the world at the time.

00:01:55.198 --> 00:01:58.701
It's difficult to illustrate how influential 
these companies were...

00:01:58.743 --> 00:01:59.828
and how much railroads

00:01:59.828 --> 00:02:02.580
were into woven into the fabric 
of American life.

00:02:03.123 --> 00:02:05.291
They were responsible for 
the temples of their time...

00:02:05.583 --> 00:02:07.710
like Penn and Grand Central stations.

00:02:08.253 --> 00:02:10.797
Also, you would not believe how amazing

00:02:10.797 --> 00:02:12.298
train travel could be back then.

00:02:12.423 --> 00:02:16.136
Sure, they had basic boring train cars
like the one I’m riding.

00:02:16.136 --> 00:02:17.637
But the upscale trains featured

00:02:17.637 --> 00:02:19.889
chandeliers, barber shops...

00:02:20.390 --> 00:02:22.600
pianos and food service

00:02:22.600 --> 00:02:25.228
that would make the Four Seasons 
look like Boston Market.

00:02:25.854 --> 00:02:28.982
One menu featured oysters, 
consommé...

00:02:29.399 --> 00:02:30.900
and celery fed duck.

00:02:31.526 --> 00:02:32.944
The bad news is my train

00:02:32.944 --> 00:02:35.488
didn't have ducks that were 
fed anything.

00:02:36.114 --> 00:02:38.741
I ate the Thai Red Curry Street noodles

00:02:38.741 --> 00:02:42.370
which, although I'm pretty sure they were 
just a microwave frozen package

00:02:42.370 --> 00:02:43.496
were actually pretty good.

00:02:44.038 --> 00:02:47.917
And although the accommodations 
were a far cry from crystal chandeliers

00:02:47.917 --> 00:02:49.043
they were comfortable enough.

00:02:51.296 --> 00:02:53.006
Good evening, ladies and gentlemen.

00:02:53.047 --> 00:02:54.841
This is the conductor speaking.

00:02:54.883 --> 00:02:57.594
It has now been 5 hours and 15 minutes

00:02:57.594 --> 00:03:01.055
which is the time it takes to fly from LAX 
to Newark Airport.

00:03:01.347 --> 00:03:04.142
And we are still in California.

00:03:05.935 --> 00:03:06.936
It's only 9:00

00:03:06.936 --> 00:03:08.521
but it already got dark outside

00:03:08.521 --> 00:03:10.231
so there's nothing to look at 
through the window...

00:03:10.648 --> 00:03:12.734
and there's no Wi-Fi, apparently.

00:03:12.734 --> 00:03:15.445
So, I guess I'm just going to 
go to sleep.

00:03:15.653 --> 00:03:17.572
They leave the overhead lights 
on all night

00:03:17.614 --> 00:03:19.991
but luckily I brought 
my trusty eye mask.

00:03:20.742 --> 00:03:23.244
I also brought some earplugs,
which should come in handy

00:03:23.244 --> 00:03:26.247
because I'm sitting right in front 
of some iPad kids

00:03:26.247 --> 00:03:29.292
who are watching movies at 
full volume with no headphones.

00:03:30.001 --> 00:03:31.211
So, that's fun.

00:03:31.544 --> 00:03:32.003
All right.

00:03:32.003 --> 00:03:33.213
I'll see you in the morning.

00:03:40.678 --> 00:03:43.890
Did I sleep well last night? Hmm?

00:03:44.891 --> 00:03:46.851
I'm going to say no.

00:03:47.227 --> 00:03:49.020
I'll spare you the details

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:51.481
but imagine getting literally 
kicked in the head 

00:03:51.481 --> 00:03:53.066
by the woman in the seat next to you.

00:03:53.316 --> 00:03:56.027
I wound up retreating to the 
observation car to sleep...

00:03:56.611 --> 00:03:57.987
which was less than ideal.

00:04:04.202 --> 00:04:05.703
Like an astronaut in space.

00:04:05.703 --> 00:04:07.622
I'm trying to move around 
as much as I can

00:04:07.622 --> 00:04:10.291
so that my muscles don't deteriorate 
during my journey.

00:04:10.792 --> 00:04:12.835
So let's talk about how American trains

00:04:12.835 --> 00:04:15.672
went from gilded pleasure palaces to...

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:16.756
This.

00:04:17.173 --> 00:04:18.591
Part of the reason why railroads 

00:04:18.591 --> 00:04:21.177
became so wildly successful 
in the first place...

00:04:21.386 --> 00:04:22.553
is because they were run by

00:04:22.553 --> 00:04:25.556
what were affectionately termed 
“robber barons”.

00:04:25.848 --> 00:04:27.684
Men like Cornelius Vanderbilt...

00:04:28.226 --> 00:04:32.397
Jay Gould, James Fisk,
and J.P. Morgan.

00:04:32.397 --> 00:04:36.401
These guys were ruthless, anti-labor,
and really corrupt...

00:04:36.693 --> 00:04:38.278
which was great when it came to building

00:04:38.278 --> 00:04:41.281
an expansive kingdom of highly profitable 
rail companies...

00:04:41.406 --> 00:04:45.034
but bad when it came to... 
just about everything else.

00:04:45.243 --> 00:04:48.538
As a result, the train companies became 
pretty universally hated

00:04:48.538 --> 00:04:49.747
by the general public.

00:04:49.998 --> 00:04:52.917
As seen in what passed for 
scathing satire at the time.

00:04:53.209 --> 00:04:54.294
“Now then, Jim.”

00:04:54.294 --> 00:04:55.628
“No jockeying, you know.”

00:04:55.920 --> 00:04:57.130
“Let em rip, Commodore.”

00:04:57.505 --> 00:04:59.340
“Don't stop to water or you'll be beat.”

00:04:59.757 --> 00:05:00.675
The federal government

00:05:00.675 --> 00:05:03.177
saw the kind of crap these guys 
were pulling and was like

00:05:03.219 --> 00:05:06.180
“Whoa, whoa, whoa, 
you can't do that.”

00:05:06.514 --> 00:05:08.599
So they instituted a bunch of regulations...

00:05:08.850 --> 00:05:10.143
which, among other things...

00:05:10.351 --> 00:05:12.478
set fixed rates for the railroad companies.

00:05:12.687 --> 00:05:13.771
And also prevented them

00:05:13.771 --> 00:05:16.024
from raising their fares or 
changing their service

00:05:16.024 --> 00:05:17.358
without the government's permission.

00:05:17.608 --> 00:05:20.236
This didn't seem like that big 
of a deal at the time...

00:05:20.486 --> 00:05:23.072
because as historian Robert Selph Henry said...

00:05:23.197 --> 00:05:25.408
“The problem , to some minds at least

00:05:25.408 --> 00:05:27.952
was how to keep railroads from 
making too much money.”

00:05:28.077 --> 00:05:30.747
After all, railroads were still by far

00:05:30.747 --> 00:05:33.916
the most efficient and the best way 
for most people to travel.

00:05:34.167 --> 00:05:36.836
This all obviously changed when 
affordable automobiles

00:05:36.836 --> 00:05:39.964
and later air travel came into the picture.

00:05:40.506 --> 00:05:42.633
So much so that by 1956

00:05:42.633 --> 00:05:45.720
only 4% of passenger travel 
was on trains.

00:05:46.054 --> 00:05:48.598
When you take into account 
that 40 years earlier

00:05:48.598 --> 00:05:50.433
that number was 98%

00:05:50.433 --> 00:05:51.934
you can see how much 
the US’s priorities

00:05:51.934 --> 00:05:54.354
had shifted when it came to travel.

00:05:54.896 --> 00:05:57.440
The train companies were far from 
a monopoly anymore...

00:05:57.732 --> 00:06:00.026
but they were still being regulated 
as if they were...

00:06:00.276 --> 00:06:01.194
which was killing them.

00:06:03.029 --> 00:06:05.490
Okay, so, right now we are being
held on the tracks

00:06:05.490 --> 00:06:06.741
to let a freight train pass.

00:06:06.991 --> 00:06:09.577
And this is actually the perfect time 
to talk about something.

00:06:09.952 --> 00:06:12.038
Private commercial passenger trains

00:06:12.038 --> 00:06:13.706
have never been very profitable.

00:06:15.416 --> 00:06:16.793
The lifeblood of the industry

00:06:16.793 --> 00:06:19.837
has always been these: 
Freight trains.

00:06:20.213 --> 00:06:22.382
But rail companies were always 
forced to provide

00:06:22.382 --> 00:06:25.009
passengers service as a benefit 
to the public.

00:06:25.051 --> 00:06:26.969
So when the rail companies began to suffer

00:06:26.969 --> 00:06:29.847
and then eventually fold, the government 
had the idea

00:06:29.847 --> 00:06:32.517
to take the responsibility for passenger service

00:06:32.517 --> 00:06:34.477
off the plates of the rail companies

00:06:34.477 --> 00:06:37.355
who could then focus on the profitable 
freight side of the business.

00:06:37.563 --> 00:06:39.315
They took all the passenger service

00:06:39.315 --> 00:06:41.109
and combined it into one company.

00:06:41.442 --> 00:06:44.362
Which is how in 1971 we got Amtrak.

00:06:44.737 --> 00:06:47.198
Which stands for the American Train Track.

00:06:47.448 --> 00:06:49.742
Again, what is up with these nicknames?

00:06:50.034 --> 00:06:52.245
“Amtrak makes it easy come, easy go

00:06:52.245 --> 00:06:54.872
to more than 450 cities in the USA.”

00:06:55.081 --> 00:06:56.416
But here is the thing.

00:06:56.791 --> 00:06:59.669
Amtrak was designed to fail 
from the beginning.

00:07:00.336 --> 00:07:03.631
That may sound ridiculous, 
but let me explain.

00:07:03.965 --> 00:07:04.924
First of all

00:07:04.924 --> 00:07:08.344
the government created Amtrak 
as a for profit corporation

00:07:08.344 --> 00:07:11.514
despite the fact that virtually 
no passenger service

00:07:11.514 --> 00:07:13.224
anywhere has been profitable.

00:07:13.516 --> 00:07:16.602
After all, the government broke off 
all the passenger trains

00:07:16.602 --> 00:07:19.313
from the rail companies because 
they weren't making money.

00:07:19.605 --> 00:07:21.399
When the government formed Amtrak

00:07:21.649 --> 00:07:25.903
they tasked it with employing innovative 
operating and marketing concepts

00:07:25.903 --> 00:07:28.781
to develop the potential of 
modern rail service.

00:07:29.157 --> 00:07:31.284
And how much did Congress give them 
to do that in their

00:07:31.284 --> 00:07:32.493
first year of operation?

00:07:33.536 --> 00:07:35.246
Just $40 million.

00:07:35.913 --> 00:07:37.957
Their budget has gone up 
a lot since then.

00:07:38.207 --> 00:07:42.211
Their budget for the year 2022 
was $2.3 billion...

00:07:42.336 --> 00:07:43.796
which sounds like a lot, doesn't it?

00:07:44.213 --> 00:07:46.757
But when you take into account that 
the federal highway budget

00:07:46.757 --> 00:07:50.094
for the same year was $64.3 billion

00:07:50.094 --> 00:07:51.637
you begin to see the inequity.

00:07:52.346 --> 00:07:54.307
To put it even more 
into perspective...

00:07:54.432 --> 00:07:57.393
the sum total of all the subsidies 
that the US

00:07:57.393 --> 00:08:00.688
has allocated to Amtrak 
in its entire history...

00:08:00.980 --> 00:08:04.442
is less than one year's funding for 
the interstate highway system.

00:08:04.859 --> 00:08:07.069
This right here shows where America's

00:08:07.069 --> 00:08:09.113
priorities lie when it comes to travel...

00:08:09.655 --> 00:08:13.618
and why Amtrak has failed 
to majorly expand or innovate.

00:08:19.165 --> 00:08:20.791
Okay, so we just got to Chicago.

00:08:20.791 --> 00:08:24.587
I got off the Amtrak about 45 hours 
after I left Los Angeles.

00:08:24.837 --> 00:08:26.255
And needless to say...

00:08:27.215 --> 00:08:29.217
I do not smell very good.

00:08:29.467 --> 00:08:32.011
I have about a 7 hour layover 
here in Chicago.

00:08:32.011 --> 00:08:34.055
And right now I'm in Union Station...

00:08:34.347 --> 00:08:36.557
which is actually kind of amazing.

00:08:43.898 --> 00:08:45.149
As you see

00:08:45.149 --> 00:08:47.109
I got a chance to put on 
some clean clothes

00:08:47.109 --> 00:08:48.319
which feels amazing.

00:08:48.611 --> 00:08:50.738
And now I'm just here 
in Millennium Park...

00:08:51.030 --> 00:08:53.658
rolling around with my suitcase 
like a dang tourist.

00:08:54.075 --> 00:08:56.160
And I'm just trying to enjoy...

00:08:56.786 --> 00:08:58.871
not being on a train 
as much as possible

00:08:58.871 --> 00:09:00.248
before I get on the second leg.

00:09:00.706 --> 00:09:02.416
The second and final leg 
of my journey.

00:09:10.216 --> 00:09:12.134
I've achieved the Holy Grail...

00:09:12.635 --> 00:09:14.428
of my own private room.

00:09:14.845 --> 00:09:17.348
I want to show you my favorite feature 
of the entire room.

00:09:17.431 --> 00:09:18.641
You're going to like this.

00:09:23.396 --> 00:09:24.814
Hidden sink.

00:09:25.314 --> 00:09:26.816
This train is a little bit more...

00:09:27.775 --> 00:09:30.236
dynamic, shall we say, 
than the last one.

00:09:31.404 --> 00:09:34.824
But I'm hoping that all 
the movements...

00:09:35.157 --> 00:09:37.535
will just help rock me to sleep.

00:09:44.625 --> 00:09:46.502
When I woke up, we had 
reached Ohio...

00:09:46.961 --> 00:09:49.255
about 2600 miles through 
my journey

00:09:49.255 --> 00:09:50.965
and 50 something hours.

00:09:52.341 --> 00:09:53.593
There are a lot of reasons

00:09:53.593 --> 00:09:55.761
why people still want to 
take the train.

00:09:56.095 --> 00:09:57.597
A big one is the environment.

00:09:57.930 --> 00:10:00.516
According to a study by 
the University of Pennsylvania...

00:10:00.808 --> 00:10:02.852
a long distance train trip produces

00:10:02.852 --> 00:10:05.605
37% less emissions 
than taking a flight.

00:10:06.147 --> 00:10:08.566
Trains are also much safer than cars.

00:10:08.941 --> 00:10:11.193
You're 17 times more likely to die

00:10:11.193 --> 00:10:13.029
while driving than 
taking a train.

00:10:13.404 --> 00:10:15.239
Flying and driving can be faster

00:10:15.239 --> 00:10:17.783
and sometimes even cheaper 
than taking the Amtrak.

00:10:18.117 --> 00:10:20.911
But they're both often 
miserable experiences.

00:10:21.203 --> 00:10:22.371
Going through TSA

00:10:22.371 --> 00:10:25.416
dealing with flight delays or 
being stuck in traffic

00:10:25.416 --> 00:10:27.001
and not being able to do anything

00:10:27.001 --> 00:10:28.753
because you need to be 
driving the car.

00:10:28.919 --> 00:10:30.087
While I've been on Amtrak

00:10:30.087 --> 00:10:32.006
I've been able to just 
stare out the window

00:10:32.006 --> 00:10:33.382
and see the country go by...

00:10:33.674 --> 00:10:36.969
or read a book or take a nap 
or whatever.

00:10:37.136 --> 00:10:38.846
I've been able to wander 
around the train

00:10:38.846 --> 00:10:40.890
whenever I want and stretch my legs.

00:10:41.015 --> 00:10:44.018
And I've had the enjoyment
of having my own private little room.

00:10:44.310 --> 00:10:46.562
And this is with Amtrak being perpetually

00:10:46.562 --> 00:10:48.439
and profoundly underfunded.

00:10:48.814 --> 00:10:51.734
Imagine what it would be like if this were evened out.

00:11:18.969 --> 00:11:20.221
So, I made it. 

00:11:20.221 --> 00:11:25.351
All total, took me about 72 hours 
and 17 minutes...

00:11:26.060 --> 00:11:28.270
which granted it would have 
taken me between

00:11:28.270 --> 00:11:30.481
5 and 6 hours to fly that distance.

00:11:31.941 --> 00:11:34.276
I don't actually know the distance
off the top of my head

00:11:34.276 --> 00:11:36.570
but I'm going to add it in post right here.

00:11:37.154 --> 00:11:38.823
But here's the thing.

00:11:39.615 --> 00:11:40.825
I had a good time.

00:11:40.950 --> 00:11:42.535
It was actually really fun.

00:11:42.535 --> 00:11:44.995
Aside from a couple of hiccups 
at the very beginning.

00:11:44.995 --> 00:11:48.332
It was a really pleasant, 
memorable experience.

00:11:48.332 --> 00:11:51.877
I got to see parts of the country
that I had never seen before.

00:11:52.378 --> 00:11:56.966
And overall, dare I say, it was 
a pleasant experience.

00:11:58.008 --> 00:11:59.385
Right now, Amtrak covers 

00:11:59.385 --> 00:12:02.763
roughly 21,000 miles of passenger rail 
in the US.

00:12:02.888 --> 00:12:05.349
But when you look at the map, 
you can see it's sparse coverage

00:12:05.349 --> 00:12:07.727
routes that don't connect...

00:12:08.144 --> 00:12:10.229
And sorry, Wyoming and South Dakota

00:12:10.229 --> 00:12:11.397
you don't get any trains.

00:12:11.731 --> 00:12:14.525
If the United States took Amtrak 
off life support...

00:12:14.817 --> 00:12:18.028
it would revolutionize the way 
people travel around the US.

00:12:18.070 --> 00:12:19.405
When he was retiring

00:12:19.405 --> 00:12:21.824
former Amtrak President Graham Claytor said...

00:12:22.074 --> 00:12:24.785
“When I came to Amtrak
I was convinced I could save it...”

00:12:25.161 --> 00:12:26.662
But finally came to the conclusion

00:12:26.662 --> 00:12:28.706
that only the American public 
can save it...

00:12:28.873 --> 00:12:30.124
“...when they say they want it.”

00:12:31.292 --> 00:12:32.418
Now, if you'll excuse me

00:12:32.418 --> 00:12:34.378
I'm going to go take a much needed shower

00:12:34.378 --> 00:12:36.297
and go to sleep 
in a real bed.

00:12:36.881 --> 00:12:39.175
I got to rest up for my flight back 
to California tomorrow.

00:12:41.927 --> 00:12:44.805
For me, one of the best parts of riding

00:12:44.805 --> 00:12:48.559
the Amtrak is just throwing on 
some Phoebe Bridgers

00:12:48.559 --> 00:12:52.062
and wistfully staring out the window 
for hours on end.

